1. Chosen Development Model

Model: Waterfall

Waterfall was chosen because the project requirements were 
clear from the start, the scope is fixed, and the DMAIC methodology follows 
the same sequential phase-by-phase structure. Each phase is fully completed 
before the next one begins — Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, and Control 
— which matches exactly how Waterfall works. The project scope is small and 
well-defined, documentation is required at each phase, and there is no need 
for changes during development.

2. User Requirements

a. Stakeholders

Bank Customers — end-users who book appointments online to avoid queuing and save time.

Bank Staff — employees who serve customers at the counter and need to manage the daily appointment queue.

Admin (Bank Manager) — system administrator who needs full control over bookings, staff accounts, and available services.

Development Team (Amela, Ester, Eriselda) — students who build and maintain the system.

b. User Stories

1. As a customer, I want to book a bank appointment online so that I can avoid waiting in a queue.
2. As a customer, I want to choose a specific date and time slot so that I can plan my visit.
3. As a customer, I want to receive a confirmation code so that I can identify my appointment at the counter.
4. As a customer, I want to view all my bookings so that I can keep track of my appointments.
5. As a customer, I want to cancel a booking so that I can free up my slot if my plans change.
6. As a customer, I want to use QueueBot so that I can get quick answers without calling the bank.
7. As a staff member, I want to see all today's appointments so that I can serve customers efficiently.
8. As a staff member, I want to mark appointments as Completed or No-show so that the system stays updated.
9. As a staff member, I want to search and filter appointments so that I can find specific bookings quickly.
10. As an admin, I want to manage staff accounts so that I can add or remove employees as needed.
11. As an admin, I want to manage banking services so that customers always see accurate options.
12. As an admin, I want to view booking analytics and export data so that I can monitor system performance.

3. Functional Requirements

a. Description

1. The system must allow customers to register with a name, email, and password.
2. The system must allow customers to log in with valid credentials.
3. The system must allow customers to log out of their account.
4. The system must display all available banking services with name, description, and duration.
5. The system must allow customers to select a service before proceeding with a booking.
6. The system must display a calendar showing available dates for booking.
7. The system must block past dates and weekends from being selected.
8. The system must display available and booked time slots for a selected date.
9. The system must prevent customers from selecting an already booked time slot.
10. The system must allow customers to enter their personal details to complete a booking.
11. The system must validate that all required fields are filled before confirming a booking.
12. The system must generate a unique QB-XXXXX confirmation code for every booking.
13. The system must display a booking summary after confirmation.
14. The system must allow customers to view all their bookings in one page.
15. The system must allow customers to cancel a confirmed booking.
16. The system must allow staff to log in with an Employee ID and password.
17. The system must allow staff to view all appointments in a table with search and filter options.
18. The system must allow staff to mark appointments as Completed or No-show.
19. The system must allow the admin to log in with separate administrator credentials.
20. The system must allow the admin to add and remove staff members.
21. The system must allow the admin to add and remove banking services.
22. The system must display booking statistics with a bar chart and a donut chart in the admin dashboard.
23. The system must allow the admin to delete any booking.
24. The system must allow the admin to export all data as a JSON file.
25. The system must include a QueueBot chatbot that answers common customer questions.

b. Acceptance Criteria

FR12 — Confirmation Code Generation
- A unique code starting with QB- is generated after every successful booking
- No two bookings have the same confirmation code
- The code is displayed on the confirmation screen immediately after booking
- The code is visible in the My Bookings page under the booking details

FR8 — Time Slot Display
- Available slots are shown after a date is selected
- Already booked slots are visually different and cannot be clicked
- Slots update correctly when a new date is selected
- All slots are within branch hours 09:00 to 17:00

FR11 — Form Validation
- Customer cannot confirm a booking if any required field is empty
- An error message is shown specifying which fields are missing
- An error message is shown if the email format is invalid
- The booking is only saved after all validations pass

FR17 — Staff Appointment Table
- All appointments are displayed with code, name, service, date, time, and status
- Staff can search by customer name, confirmation code, or service
- Staff can filter by All, Confirmed, Completed, Cancelled, or No-show
- Results update immediately when a search or filter is applied

FR22 — Admin Analytics Dashboard
- The bar chart shows the number of bookings grouped by service
- The donut chart shows the percentage breakdown of booking statuses
- Both charts update automatically when booking data changes
- The total number of bookings is shown in a KPI card above the charts

4. Non-Functional Requirements

a. Descriptions

1. The system must load all pages in under 2 seconds.
2. The booking process must complete in under 5 seconds from start to confirmation.
3. The system must work correctly on Chrome, Safari, and Firefox browsers.
4. The system must display correctly on desktop, tablet, and mobile screens.
5. The system must save booking data permanently in the database so it is never lost.
6. The system must handle multiple bookings in the same session without errors.
7. The system must be easy to use for customers with no technical experience.
8. All buttons, labels, and instructions must be clearly readable.
9. The color contrast must meet basic readability standards.
10. The booking flow must be completable in 3 steps or fewer without instructions.
11. The staff dashboard must only be accessible after a valid staff login.
12. The admin panel must only be accessible after valid admin credentials.
13. No customer must be able to access the staff or admin pages directly.
14. API credentials must be stored only in the server file and never exposed in the browser.
15. All form inputs must be validated before any data is saved.
16. The system must display error messages clearly when something goes wrong.
17. The QueueBot must respond to customer questions within 1 second.
18. The calendar must render available slots instantly after a date is selected.
19. The system code must be organized across separate HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files.
20. Each file must handle only its own page logic to make updates easy.
21. The admin data export must generate a complete and accurate JSON file.
22. The system must work without an internet connection for all features except QueueBot.

b. Acceptance Criteria

NFR1 — Page Load Speed
- All four pages load in under 2 seconds on a standard connection
- The booking confirmation screen appears in under 1 second after submitting
- The calendar renders instantly when the booking page is opened
- No page shows a blank screen for more than 1 second

NFR4 — Responsiveness
- The home page displays correctly on screens smaller than 768px
- The booking steps stack vertically on mobile screens
- All buttons are large enough to tap on a touchscreen
- The staff and admin tables scroll horizontally on small screens

NFR7 — Usability
- A first-time user can complete a booking without any instructions
- The 4-step progress indicator is always visible during the booking flow
- Error messages appear next to the field that caused the error
- The QueueBot suggestion buttons guide users without them needing to type

NFR11 — Staff Access Security
- The Staff Dashboard page shows only the login screen before authentication
- Entering a wrong Employee ID or password shows an error message
- The dashboard data is only visible after successful login
- Staff cannot navigate to the admin panel from the staff portal

NFR19 — Code Maintainability
- Each page has its own separate HTML, CSS, and JavaScript file
- No page's JavaScript file contains logic belonging to another page
- CSS styles are not duplicated across files
- A new developer can understand the file structure within 10 minutes

5. Application Specifications

a. Architecture

QueueBank MS follows a three-tier architecture. The Presentation Layer consists 
of four HTML pages — Home, User Portal, Staff Dashboard, and Admin Panel — each
styled with CSS. The Logic Layer consists of JavaScript files that handle all 
interactivity including the booking flow, calendar, form validation, and data 
management, along with PHP files that process requests and communicate with 
the database. The Data Layer uses a MySQL database running on XAMPP to store 
all booking, staff, and service data permanently and consistently across all 
users and devices. For the QueueBot feature, a lightweight Node.js server built
with Express acts as middleware between the browser and the AI service.

b. Database Model

QueueBank MS uses MySQL as its data store organized into three tables.

Bookings — stores confirmation code, service name and duration, date and
time slot, customer name, email, phone, notes, booking status, and created 
timestamp. Each booking code is unique and each time slot can only be booked once per date.

Staff — stores Employee ID, name, email, role, and status. Each Employee ID must be unique.

Services — stores service ID, icon, name, description, and duration in minutes. All bookings 
reference a valid service ID.

c. Technologies Used

- HTML5 — page structure, works in all browsers without installation
- CSS3 — styling and responsive design, full control over the pastel green theme
- JavaScript — all interactivity and booking logic, runs directly in the browser
- PHP — server-side scripting that connects the website to the MySQL database
- MySQL — relational database that stores all bookings, staff, and services permanently
and allows all users to share the same data across different devices and browsers
- XAMPP — local server environment that runs Apache, PHP, and MySQL together
- Node.js and Express — QueueBot backend server, lightweight and easy to run locally
- Google Fonts — Playfair Display and Nunito for professional typography
- SVG — custom illustrations, scalable with no external image files needed

d. User Interface Design

The interface uses a pastel green color palette with sage green, mint, teal, 
and cream as the main colors. Typography uses Playfair Display for headings 
and Nunito for body text.

Home Page — public landing page with a hero section featuring a custom illustrated
bank scene showing a teller serving a customer, two people waiting on a bench, 
a wall clock, and a calendar. Includes a stats bar, a How It Works section with
four illustrated steps, and portal selection cards for Customer, Staff, and Admin.

User Portal — two-tab layout with Book Appointment and My Bookings. The booking
flow uses a four-step wizard with a visible step indicator: choose a service, 
pick a date and time slot, enter personal details, and view the confirmation screen
with a unique code.

Staff Dashboard — protected by a login screen. After login the page shows KPI cards, 
today's appointment stats, and a full appointment table with search, filter, and action 
buttons.

Admin Panel — protected by a separate login with a sidebar navigation. Sections include
Dashboard with charts, All Bookings, Staff Management, User Records, Services Management,
and System Settings.

